# TLS server, CHUNKING reception with TLS shutdown mid-chunk
gnutls
#
# A TLS session that goes away while a BDAT chunk is still being read leaves the
# server holding readers, and a transfer buffer, belonging to a session that has
# ceased to exist.  Reading has to stop there.  Going on would either reach into
# the released buffer or read the rest of the connection in clear, on the socket
# the peer negotiated encryption for.
#
# Four sessions below, each aimed at a different consequence of that:
#
#   1  the shutdown lands in the body, and the byte still owed on the chunk is
#      offered in clear afterwards.  A server that carries on reading takes it,
#      finishes the chunk, and pushes a byte back through the saved readers into
#      the released transfer buffer.
#   2  the same shutdown with nothing offered afterwards, so the close is clean
#      and can be asserted as such.  Session 1 cannot assert it, because the
#      byte it leaves unconsumed makes the close carry a reset.
#   3  the shutdown lands inside the header block, where the end of the input is
#      reached during the look-ahead for a continued header line.  Unless that
#      end is visible through the reader that teardown installs, the part of the
#      message that did arrive is taken for a whole one and accepted.
#   4  the shutdown lands at a command boundary, between messages, which is what
#      a sending process does when it hands the connection to its successor.
#      That is allowed to go on in clear - but as a plain connection which has
#      authenticated nothing and negotiated nothing, never as a continuation
#      carrying what the session that has gone established.
#
# The first daemon runs with the receive debug selected, so the reader bookkeeping
# the chunked path performs is on the record: a session that pushed or popped the
# saved readers out of turn would say so there, and the saved output would no
# longer match.  Session 4 gets a daemon of its own, for the reason given where it
# is started.
#
# Note for running this by hand: the daemon under test has to be the GnuTLS
# build.  Hand that binary to runtest and leave --tls off - that is the case in
# which the binary runtest was given is used at both ends, as $exim_client and
# as $exim_server alike.  Given --tls, runtest works in its cross-library mode
# instead: it picks the harness client program, and it also pairs the two
# library-specific builds against each other, so "--tls gnutls" leaves the
# OpenSSL build answering as $exim_server - and that build never frees the
# transfer buffer this test is about.  Test numbers spanning more than one
# script directory have to be asked for as a range, or one batch per directory.
#
exim -d-all+receive -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
#
# 1: shutdown in the body, with the outstanding byte offered in clear.
#
# Chunk arithmetic - 85 declared, 84 sent, so one byte is still outstanding when
# the session goes.  The ">>> " prefix suppresses the trailing CRLF the client
# would otherwise add, so the payload is exactly the text after it:
#
#     To: Susan@random.com CRLF      22
#     From: Sam@random.com CRLF      22   (44)
#     CRLF, ending the header block   2   (46)
#     body-start-                    11   (57)
#     27 x A                         27   (84)
#
# Both halves of that shape are load-bearing.  The header block has to be
# complete, so that reception is past the header lines and into the body: that is
# where the end of a chunk is handled by pushing a byte back through the saved
# readers, which writes into the transfer buffer.  And the chunk has to be short
# by exactly one byte, that byte then being offered in clear once the session has
# gone - a server that still reads the plain socket takes it, finishes the chunk,
# and performs the push-back.  Leave either half out and the reader never gets
# that far, so a build carrying the fault would look every bit as healthy as one
# without it.
#
# The reply is matched in full rather than as just "421" because the receive
# timeout answers with a 421 of its own, and a loose match would be satisfied by
# the very behaviour this test exists to reject.
#
# The connection is not read again after the reply.  The byte offered in clear is
# deliberately never consumed, so the close that follows carries a reset, and a
# further read would report that reset rather than end of file.  Session 2 is
# what asserts the close.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
