#
# 3: shutdown inside the header block.
#
# 88 declared, 44 sent - two complete header lines and no blank line, so the
# header block is still open when the session goes:
#
#     To: Susan@random.com CRLF      22
#     From: Sam@random.com CRLF      22   (44)
#
# Header reading asks for the next byte to find out whether the line continues.
# That request reaches the end of the input, which the reader installed at
# teardown has to report as such; a reader that does not is understood to have
# reached the end of the message instead, and the part of the message that did
# arrive is accepted as a message in its own right - answered as the whole
# declared chunk, and logged as received over the session.  So the log must record
# the loss after the header, and must record no acceptance.  The reply carries no
# distinction - it is the same lost-connection one as above - so it is the log
# that tells the two points of loss apart.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
