#
# The three sessions above are the ones the reader bookkeeping has to be read for,
# and their children are short-lived enough that the daemon's own notes about them
# always land after they have finished.  Session 4's child stays for a second
# message, and the daemon's notes then land in the middle of it - at no fixed
# place, because it depends on which of the two the system runs first.  So the
# daemon for session 4 is started without the debug: nothing of it reaches the
# saved output, and what the sessions above have to say about the readers is left
# unaffected by it.
#
exim -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
millisleep 500
#
# 4: shutdown at a command boundary.
#
# A whole message is sent and accepted over the session first, so that nothing is
# in progress when the shutdown arrives.  The conversation then continues in
# clear, which is what a sending process expects when it shuts the session down
# to hand the connection on.  The second message is therefore accepted - and the
# log line for it is the assertion: it has to be a plain one.  Compare it with
# the first message's line, which carries P=esmtps and the cipher: the second
# carries P=esmtp and no cipher, no certificate verification, no authentication.
# Anything of the session appearing on that line would mean the connection had
# kept, in clear, what was established under encryption.
#
# STARTTLS is offered again after the shutdown, which is correct: there is no
# session on this connection any more, and a successor is free to start one.
#
# The second message is sent chunked as well, so the whole test stays on the
# chunked path: 49 bytes, being the subject line and the blank line that closes
# the header block, each with its CRLF.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
